#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"From: Junio C Hamano <gitster@pobox.com>\n"
"Date: Wed, 21 Nov 2007 16:32:55 -0800\n"
"Subject: Addendum to \"MaintNotes\"\n"
"Abstract: Imagine that Git development is racing along as usual, when our "
"friendly\n"
" neighborhood maintainer is struck down by a wayward bus. Out of the\n"
" hordes of suckers (loyal developers), you have been tricked (chosen) to\n"
" step up as the new maintainer. This howto will show you \"how to\" do it.\n"
"Content-type: text/asciidoc\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to maintain Git"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "Activities"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:17
msgid "The maintainer's Git time is spent on three activities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:19
msgid "Communication (45%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:23
msgid ""
"Mailing list discussions on general design, fielding user questions, "
"diagnosing bug reports; reviewing, commenting on, suggesting alternatives "
"to, and rejecting patches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:25
msgid "Integration (50%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:30
msgid ""
"Applying new patches from the contributors while spotting and correcting "
"minor mistakes, shuffling the integration and testing branches, pushing the "
"results out, cutting the releases, and making announcements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:32
msgid "Own development (5%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:34
msgid "Scratching my own itch and sending proposed patch series out."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Policy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:41
msgid ""
"The policy on Integration is informally mentioned in \"A Note from the "
"maintainer\" message, which is periodically posted to the mailing list after "
"each feature release is made:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:45
msgid ""
"Feature releases are numbered as vX.Y.0 and are meant to contain bugfixes "
"and enhancements in any area, including functionality, performance and "
"usability, without regression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:49
msgid ""
"Maintenance releases are numbered as vX.Y.Z (0 < Z) and are meant to contain "
"only bugfixes for the corresponding vX.Y.0 feature release and earlier "
"maintenance releases vX.Y.W (W < Z)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:54
msgid ""
"The 'master' branch is used to prepare for the next feature release. In "
"other words, at some point, the tip of 'master' branch is tagged as "
"vX.(Y+1).0, when vX.Y.0 is the latest feature release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:60
msgid ""
"'maint' branch is used to prepare for the next maintenance release.  After "
"the feature release vX.Y.0 is made, the tip of 'maint' branch is set to that "
"release, and bugfixes will accumulate on the branch, and at some point, the "
"tip of the branch is tagged with vX.Y.1, vX.Y.2, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:68
msgid ""
"'next' branch is used to publish changes (both enhancements and fixes) that "
"(1) have worthwhile goal, (2) are in a fairly good shape suitable for "
"everyday use, (3) but have not yet demonstrated to be regression free.  "
"Reviews from contributors on the mailing list help to make the "
"determination.  After a topic is merged to 'next', it is tested for at least "
"7 calendar days before getting merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:73
msgid ""
"'seen' branch is used to publish other proposed changes that do not yet pass "
"the criteria set for 'next' (see above), but there is no promise that 'seen' "
"will contain everything.  A topic that had no reviewer reaction may not be "
"picked up."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:77
msgid ""
"A new topic will first get merged to 'seen', unless it is trivially correct "
"and clearly urgent, in which case it may be directly merged to 'next' or "
"even to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:86
msgid ""
"If a topic that was picked up to 'seen' becomes and stays inactive for 3 "
"calendar weeks without having seen a clear consensus that it is good enough "
"to be moved to 'next', the topic may be discarded from 'seen'.  Interested "
"parties are still free to revive the topic.  For the purpose of this "
"guideline, the definition of being \"inactive\" is that nobody has discussed "
"the topic, no new iteration of the topic was posted, and no responses to the "
"review comments were given."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:92
msgid ""
"The tips of 'master' and 'maint' branches will not be rewound to allow "
"people to build their own customization on top of them.  Early in a new "
"development cycle, 'next' is rewound to the tip of 'master' once, but "
"otherwise it will not be rewound until the end of the cycle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:96
msgid ""
"Usually 'master' contains all of 'maint' and 'next' contains all of "
"'master'.  'seen' contains all the topics merged to 'next', but is rebuilt "
"directly on 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:99
msgid ""
"The tip of 'master' is meant to be more stable than any tagged releases, and "
"the users are encouraged to follow it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:103
msgid ""
"The 'next' branch is where new action takes place, and the users are "
"encouraged to test it so that regressions and bugs are found before new "
"topics are merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:110
msgid ""
"When a problem is found in a topic in 'next', the topic is marked not to be "
"merged to 'master'.  Follow-up patches are discussed on the mailing list and "
"applied to the topic after being reviewed and then the topic is merged "
"(again) to 'next'.  After going through the usual testing in 'next', the "
"entire (fixed) topic is merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:116
msgid ""
"One release cycle for a feature release is expected to last for eight to ten "
"weeks.  A few \"release candidate\" releases are expected to be tagged about "
"a week apart before the final release, and a \"preview\" release is tagged "
"about a week before the first release candidate gets tagged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:122
msgid ""
"After the preview release is tagged, topics that were well reviewed may be "
"merged to 'master' before spending the usual 7 calendar days in 'next', with "
"the expectation that any bugs in them can be caught and fixed in the release "
"candidates before the final release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:129
msgid ""
"After the first release candidate is tagged, the contributors are strongly "
"encouraged to focus on finding and fixing new regressions introduced during "
"the cycle, over addressing old bugs and any new features.  Topics stop "
"getting merged down from 'next' to 'master', and new topics stop getting "
"merged to 'next'. Unless they are fixes to new regressions in the cycle, "
"that is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:134
msgid ""
"Soon after a feature release is made, the tip of 'maint' gets fast-forwarded "
"to point at the release.  Topics that have been kept in 'next' are merged "
"down to 'master' and a new development cycle starts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:139
msgid ""
"Note that before v1.9.0 release, the version numbers used to be structured "
"slightly differently.  vX.Y.Z were feature releases while vX.Y.Z.W were "
"maintenance releases for vX.Y.Z."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:147
msgid ""
"Because most of the lines of code in Git are written by individual "
"contributors, and contributions come in the form of e-mailed patches "
"published on the mailing list, the project maintains a mapping from "
"individual commits to the Message-Id of the e-mail that resulted in the "
"commit, to help tracking the origin of the changes. The notes in "
"\"refs/notes/amlog\" are used for this purpose, and are published along with "
"the broken-out branches to the maintainer's repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Typical Git Day"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:153
msgid ""
"A typical Git day for the maintainer implements the above policy by doing "
"the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:159
msgid ""
"Scan mailing list.  Respond with review comments, suggestions etc.  Kibitz.  "
"Collect potentially usable patches from the mailing list.  Patches about a "
"single topic go to one mailbox (I read my mail in Gnus, and type \\C-o to "
"save/append messages in files in mbox format)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:164
msgid ""
"Write his own patches to address issues raised on the list but nobody has "
"stepped up to solve.  Send it out just like other contributors do, and pick "
"them up just like patches from other contributors (see above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:169
msgid ""
"Review the patches in the saved mailboxes.  Edit proposed log message for "
"typofixes and clarifications, and add Acks collected from the list.  Edit "
"patch to incorporate \"Oops, that should have been like this\" fixes from "
"the discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:172
msgid "Classify the collected patches and handle 'master' and 'maint' updates:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:174
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:189
msgid ""
"Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'maint' are directly "
"applied to 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:176
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:191
msgid ""
"Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'master' are directly "
"applied to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:177
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:192
msgid "Other topics are not handled in this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:179
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:194
msgid "This step is done with \"git am\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:183
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout master    ;# or \"git checkout maint\"\n"
" $ git am -sc3 mailbox\n"
" $ make test\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:186
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:201
msgid "In practice, almost no patch directly goes to 'master' or 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:205
msgid ""
"Applying the e-mailed patches using \"git am\" automatically records the "
"mappings from 'Message-Id' to the applied commit in the \"amlog\" "
"notes. Periodically check that this is working with \"git show -s "
"--notes=amlog $commit\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:211
msgid ""
"This mapping is maintained with the aid of the \"post-applypatch\" hook "
"found in the 'todo' branch. That hook should be installed before applying "
"patches. It is also helpful to carry forward any relevant amlog entries when "
"rebasing, so the following config may be useful:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" [notes]\n"
" \trewriteRef = refs/notes/amlog\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:218
msgid ""
"Avoid \"cherry-pick\", as it does not propagate notes by design. Use either "
"\"git commit --amend\" or \"git rebase\" to make corrections to an existing "
"commit, even for a single-patch topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:222
msgid ""
"Make sure that a push refspec for 'refs/notes/amlog' is in the remote "
"configuration for publishing repositories. A few sample configurations look "
"like the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:227
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" [remote \"github\"]\n"
" \turl = https://github.com/gitster/git\n"
" \tpushurl = github.com:gitster/git.git\n"
" \tmirror\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:237
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" [remote \"github2\"]\n"
" \turl = https://github.com/git/git\n"
" \tfetch = +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/github2/*\n"
" \tpushurl = github.com:git/git.git\n"
" \tpush = refs/heads/maint:refs/heads/maint\n"
" \tpush = refs/heads/master:refs/heads/master\n"
" \tpush = refs/heads/next:refs/heads/next\n"
" \tpush = +refs/heads/seen:refs/heads/seen\n"
" \tpush = +refs/notes/amlog\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:242
msgid ""
"Review the last issue of \"What's cooking\" message, review the topics ready "
"for merging (topic->master and topic->maint).  Use \"Meta/cook -w\" script "
"(where Meta/ contains a checkout of the 'todo' branch) to aid this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:245
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:256
msgid ""
"And perform the merge.  Use \"Meta/Reintegrate -e\" script (see later) to "
"aid this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:247
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:258
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ Meta/cook -w last-issue-of-whats-cooking.mbox\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:253
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:264
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout master    ;# or \"git checkout maint\"\n"
" $ echo ai/topic | Meta/Reintegrate -e ;# \"git merge ai/topic\"\n"
" $ git log -p ORIG_HEAD.. ;# final review\n"
" $ git diff ORIG_HEAD..   ;# final review\n"
" $ make test              ;# final review\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:268
msgid ""
"If the tip of 'master' is updated, also generate the preformatted "
"documentation and push the out result to git-htmldocs and git-manpages "
"repositories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:270
msgid "Handle the remaining patches:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:279
msgid ""
"Anything unobvious that is applicable to 'master' (in other words, does not "
"depend on anything that is still in 'next' and not in 'master') is applied "
"to a new topic branch that is forked from the tip of 'master' (or the last "
"feature release, which is a bit older than 'master').  This includes both "
"enhancements and unobvious fixes to 'master'.  A topic branch is named as "
"ai/topic where \"ai\" is two-letter string named after author's initial and "
"\"topic\" is a descriptive name of the topic (in other words, \"what's the "
"series is about\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:283
msgid ""
"An unobvious fix meant for 'maint' is applied to a new topic branch that is "
"forked from the tip of 'maint' (or the oldest and still relevant maintenance "
"branch).  The topic may be named as ai/maint-topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:285
msgid "Changes that pertain to an existing topic are applied to the branch, but:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:286
msgid "obviously correct ones are applied first;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:287
msgid "questionable ones are discarded or applied to near the tip;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:289
msgid ""
"Replacement patches to an existing topic are accepted only for commits not "
"in 'next'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:291
msgid "The initial round is done with:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:294
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout ai/topic ;# or \"git checkout -b ai/topic master\"\n"
" $ git am -sc3 --whitespace=warn mailbox\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:296
msgid "and replacing an existing topic with subsequent round is done with:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout master...ai/topic ;# try to reapply to the same base\n"
" $ git am -sc3 --whitespace=warn mailbox\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:301
msgid "to prepare the new round on a detached HEAD, and then"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:304
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git range-diff @{-1}...\n"
" $ git diff @{-1}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:306
msgid "to double check what changed since the last round, and finally"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:308
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ git checkout -B @{-1}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:311
msgid ""
"to conclude (the last step is why a topic already in 'next' is not replaced "
"but updated incrementally)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:325
msgid ""
"Whether it is the initial round or a subsequent round, the topic may not "
"build even in isolation, or may break the build when merged to integration "
"branches due to bugs.  There may already be obvious and trivial improvements "
"suggested on the list.  The maintainer often adds an extra commit, with "
"\"SQUASH???\" in its title, to fix things up, before publishing the "
"integration branches to make it usable by other developers for testing.  "
"These changes are what the maintainer is not 100% committed to (trivial "
"typofixes etc. are often squashed directly into the patches that need "
"fixing, without being applied as a separate \"SQUASH???\" commit), so that "
"they can be removed easily as needed.  The expectation is that the original "
"author will make corrections in a reroll."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:329
msgid ""
"By now, new topic branches are created and existing topic branches are "
"updated.  The integration branches 'next', 'jch', and 'seen' need to be "
"updated to contain them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:332
msgid ""
"If there are topics that have been merged to 'master' and should be merged "
"to 'maint', merge them to 'maint', and update the release notes to the next "
"maintenance release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:336
msgid ""
"Review the latest issue of \"What's cooking\" again.  Are topics that have "
"been sufficiently long in 'next' ready to be merged to 'master'? Are topics "
"we saw earlier and are in 'seen' now got positive reviews and are ready to "
"be merged to 'next'?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:339
msgid ""
"If there are topics that have been cooking in 'next' long enough and should "
"be merged to 'master', merge them to 'master', and update the release notes "
"to the next feature release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:341
msgid ""
"If there were patches directly made on 'maint', merge 'maint' to 'master'; "
"make sure that the result is what you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git checkout master\n"
"$ git merge -m \"Sync with 'maint'\" --no-log maint\n"
"$ git log -p --first-parent ORIG_HEAD..\n"
"$ make test\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:350
msgid ""
"Prepare to update the 'jch' branch, which is used to represent somewhere "
"between 'master' and 'seen' and often is slightly ahead of 'next', and the "
"'seen' branch, which is used to hold the rest."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:352
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ Meta/Reintegrate master..jch >Meta/redo-jch.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:355
msgid ""
"The result is a script that lists topics to be merged in order to rebuild "
"the current 'jch'.  Do the same for 'seen'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:360
msgid ""
"Review the Meta/redo-jch.sh and Meta/redo-seen.sh scripts.  The former "
"should have a line '### match next'---the idea is that merging the topics "
"listed before the line on top of 'master' should result in a tree identical "
"to that of 'next'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:367
msgid ""
"As newly created topics are usually merged near the tip of 'seen', add them "
"to the end of the Meta/redo-seen.sh script.  Among the topics that were in "
"'seen', there may be ones that are not quite ready for 'next' but are "
"getting there.  Move them from Meta/redo-seen.sh to the end of "
"Meta/redo-jch.sh.  The expectation is that you'd use 'jch' as your daily "
"driver as the first guinea pig, so you should choose carefully."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:370
msgid ""
"Now we are ready to start rebuilding 'jch' and merging topics to 'next'.  "
"For each branch whose tip is not merged to 'next', one of three things can "
"happen:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:371
msgid "The commits are all next-worthy; merge the topic to next;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:373
msgid ""
"The new parts are of mixed quality, but earlier ones are next-worthy; merge "
"the early parts to next;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:374
msgid "Nothing is next-worthy; do not do anything."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:380
msgid ""
"This step is aided with Meta/redo-jch.sh script created earlier.  If a topic "
"that was already in 'next' gained a patch, the script would list it as "
"\"ai/topic~1\".  To include the new patch to the updated 'next', drop the "
"\"~1\" part; to keep it excluded, do not touch the line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:383
msgid ""
"If a topic that was not in 'next' should be merged to 'next', add it before "
"the '### match next' line.  Then:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:386
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout --detach master\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -c1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:390
msgid ""
"to rebuild the 'jch' branch from scratch.  \"-c1\" tells the script to stop "
"merging at the first line that begins with '###' (i.e. the \"### match "
"next\" line you added earlier)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:397
msgid ""
"At this point, build-test the result.  It may reveal semantic conflicts "
"(e.g. a topic renamed a variable, another added a new reference to the "
"variable under its old name), in which case prepare an appropriate merge-fix "
"first (see appendix), and rebuild the 'jch' branch from scratch, starting at "
"the tip of 'master', this time without using \"-c1\" to merge all topics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:399
msgid "Then do the same to 'next'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:402
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout next\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -c1 -e\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:409
msgid ""
"The \"-e\" option allows the merge message that comes from the history of "
"the topic and the comments in the \"What's cooking\" to be edited.  The "
"resulting tree should match 'jch^{/^### match next'}' as the same set of "
"topics are merged on 'master'; otherwise there is a mismerge. Investigate "
"why and do not proceed until the mismerge is found and rectified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:411
msgid "If 'master' was updated before you started redoing 'next', then"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:413
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ git diff 'jch^{/^### match next}' next\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:417
msgid ""
"would show differences that went into 'master' (which 'jch' has, but 'next' "
"does not yet---often it is updates to the release notes).  Merge 'master' "
"back to 'next' if that is the case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:419
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ git merge -m \"Sync with 'master'\" --no-log master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:421
msgid "When all is well, clean up the redo-jch.sh script with"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:423
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -u\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:427
msgid ""
"This removes topics listed in the script that have already been merged to "
"'master'.  This may lose '### match next' marker; add it again to the "
"appropriate place when it happens."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:429
msgid "Rebuild 'seen' on top of 'jch'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git checkout -B seen jch\n"
"$ sh Meta/redo-seen.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:434
msgid "When all is well, clean up the redo-seen.sh script with"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:436
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ sh Meta/redo-seen.sh -u\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:438
msgid "Double check by running"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:440
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ git branch --no-merged seen '??/*'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:443
msgid ""
"Update \"What's cooking\" message to review the updates to existing topics, "
"newly added topics and graduated topics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:445
msgid "This step is helped with Meta/cook script."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:447
#, no-wrap
msgid " $ Meta/cook\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:457
msgid ""
"This script inspects the history between master..seen, finds tips of topic "
"branches, compares what it found with the current contents in "
"Meta/whats-cooking.txt, and updates that file.  Topics not listed in the "
"file but are found in master..seen are added to the \"New topics\" section, "
"topics listed in the file that are no longer found in master..seen are moved "
"to the \"Graduated to master\" section, and topics whose commits changed "
"their states (e.g. used to be only in 'seen', now merged to 'next') are "
"updated with change markers \"<<\" and \">>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:463
msgid ""
"Look for lines enclosed in \"<<\" and \">>\"; they hold contents from old "
"file that are replaced by this integration round.  After verifying them, "
"remove the old part.  Review the description for each topic and update its "
"doneness and plan as needed.  To review the updated plan, run"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:465
#, no-wrap
msgid "     $ Meta/cook -w\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:469
msgid ""
"which will pick up comments given to the topics, such as \"Will merge to "
"'next'\", etc. (see Meta/cook script to learn what kind of phrases are "
"supported)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:472
msgid ""
"Compile, test and install all four (five) integration branches; Meta/Dothem "
"script may aid this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:475
msgid ""
"Format documentation if the 'master' branch was updated; Meta/dodoc.sh "
"script may aid this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:478
msgid ""
"Push the integration branches out to public places; Meta/pushall script may "
"aid this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:480
#, no-wrap
msgid "Observations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:483
msgid "Some observations to be made."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:487
msgid ""
"Each topic is tested individually, and also together with other topics "
"cooking first in 'seen', then in 'jch' and then in 'next'.  Until it "
"matures, no part of it is merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:492
msgid ""
"A topic already in 'next' can get fixes while still in 'next'.  Such a topic "
"will have many merges to 'next' (in other words, \"git log --first-parent "
"next\" will show many \"Merge branch 'ai/topic' to next\" for the same "
"topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:496
msgid ""
"An unobvious fix for 'maint' is cooked in 'next' and then merged to 'master' "
"to make extra sure it is Ok and then merged to 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:501
msgid ""
"Even when 'next' becomes empty (in other words, all topics prove stable and "
"are merged to 'master' and \"git diff master next\" shows empty), it has "
"tons of merge commits that will never be in 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:505
msgid ""
"In principle, \"git log --first-parent master..next\" should show nothing "
"but merges (in practice, there are fixup commits and reverts that are not "
"merges)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:509
msgid ""
"Commits near the tip of a topic branch that are not in 'next' are fair game "
"to be discarded, replaced or rewritten.  Commits already merged to 'next' "
"will not be."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:513
msgid ""
"Being in the 'next' branch is not a guarantee for a topic to be included in "
"the next feature release.  Being in the 'master' branch typically is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:521
msgid ""
"Due to the nature of \"SQUASH???\" fix-ups, if the original author agrees "
"with the suggested changes, it is OK to squash them to appropriate patches "
"in the next round (when the suggested change is small enough, the author "
"should not even bother with \"Helped-by\").  It is also OK to drop them from "
"the next round when the original author does not agree with the suggestion, "
"but the author is expected to say why somewhere in the discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:524
#, no-wrap
msgid "Appendix"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:527
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preparing a \"merge-fix\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:535
msgid ""
"A merge of two topics may not textually conflict but still have conflict at "
"the semantic level. A classic example is for one topic to rename a variable "
"and all its uses, while another topic adds a new use of the variable under "
"its old name. When these two topics are merged together, the reference to "
"the variable newly added by the latter topic will still use the old name in "
"the result."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:541
msgid ""
"The Meta/Reintegrate script that is used by redo-jch and redo-seen scripts "
"implements a crude but usable way to work around this issue.  When the "
"script merges branch $X, it checks if \"refs/merge-fix/$X\" exists, and if "
"so, the effect of it is squashed into the result of the mechanical merge.  "
"In other words,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:543
#, no-wrap
msgid "     $ echo $X | Meta/Reintegrate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:545
msgid "is roughly equivalent to this sequence:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git merge --rerere-autoupdate $X\n"
"     $ git commit\n"
"     $ git cherry-pick -n refs/merge-fix/$X\n"
"     $ git commit --amend\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:554
msgid ""
"The goal of this \"prepare a merge-fix\" step is to come up with a commit "
"that can be squashed into a result of mechanical merge to correct semantic "
"conflicts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:560
msgid ""
"After finding that the result of merging branch \"ai/topic\" to an "
"integration branch had such a semantic conflict, say seen~4, check the "
"problematic merge out on a detached HEAD, edit the working tree to fix the "
"semantic conflict, and make a separate commit to record the fix-up:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git checkout seen~4\n"
"     $ git show -s --pretty=%s ;# double check\n"
"     Merge branch 'ai/topic' to seen\n"
"     $ edit\n"
"     $ git commit -m 'merge-fix/ai/topic' -a\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:569
msgid "Then make a reference \"refs/merge-fix/ai/topic\" to point at this result:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:571
#, no-wrap
msgid "     $ git update-ref refs/merge-fix/ai/topic HEAD\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:574
msgid "Then double check the result by asking Meta/Reintegrate to redo the merge:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git checkout seen~5 ;# the parent of the problem merge\n"
"     $ echo ai/topic | Meta/Reintegrate\n"
"     $ git diff seen~4\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:582
msgid ""
"This time, because you prepared refs/merge-fix/ai/topic, the resulting merge "
"should have been tweaked to include the fix for the semantic conflict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:594
msgid ""
"Note that this assumes that the order in which conflicting branches are "
"merged does not change.  If the reason why merging ai/topic branch needs "
"this merge-fix is because another branch merged earlier to the integration "
"branch changed the underlying assumption ai/topic branch made (e.g. ai/topic "
"branch added a site to refer to a variable, while the other branch renamed "
"that variable and adjusted existing use sites), and if you changed redo-jch "
"(or redo-seen) script to merge ai/topic branch before the other branch, then "
"the above merge-fix should not be applied while merging ai/topic, but should "
"instead be applied while merging the other branch.  You would need to move "
"the fix to apply to the other branch, perhaps like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/howto/maintain-git.txt:597
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      $ mf=refs/merge-fix\n"
"      $ git update-ref $mf/$the_other_branch $mf/ai/topic\n"
"      $ git update-ref -d $mf/ai/topic\n"
msgstr ""
